WEBVTT

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.800
This is Coast!

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:43.920
One small sea lies at the heart of our islands... ..and touches us all.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:52.120
We've been crisscrossing it for centuries to connect with coastal neighbours.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:04.000
Bounded by Scotland in the north, England in the east, Wales in the south and Ireland in the west, I'm on the Irish Sea.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:17.560
As I head across the sea to Ireland, we're also exploring other shores around the Irish Sea.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.000
Mark's digging up shared Celtic connections in Cumbria...

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:29.200
I'm on a quest to discover why this outpost of the Roman Empire was so important as a religious centre.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.880
..Cassie and Miranda are diving into a shipwreck mystery...

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.600
There are just eerie bits of wreckage lying around.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:47.640
..And Tessa's investigating how one small yacht determined the future of Ireland...

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.080
On-board is an illegal cargo.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.520
900 of these...

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.360
..Mauser single shot rifles.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:11.360
..All aboard the Irish Sea.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:44.000
40,000 square miles of water touching the shores of all four nations that make up the British Isles.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:03:04.040
For thousands of years people have traversed the Irish Sea from coast to coast, creating a special shared culture.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:10.920
I want to find the many ways this swirling sea touches our isles and connects our coast.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.800
This passage of water is awash with stories I'm itching to explore.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.280
I'm starting my journey on a crossing that has long linked our islands.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:33.440
From there, I'll head north for Belfast and then Larne, where I'll hop on another ferry to Cairnryan in Scotland.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:46.640
Over five million people cross the Irish Sea each year, but how many know where it starts and ends?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.920
I asked some fellow passengers before we set sail.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.040
I think, actually, it goes right, right up.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.200
Right up to top of Ireland?

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.640
Yeah.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.040
I would say it stops round about here.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.640
Across there?

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:56.800
Yup.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.040
In the north, yeah?

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.560
And how far down do you think it goes?

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.800
Probably down...down about here, I suppose.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.480
So all the sea...

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.720
It joins the Channel down there.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.560
All the sea that's between mainland Britain and Ireland, you think is the Irish Sea?

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:15.880
I think so, yeah.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:27.840
On sea charts, the Irish Sea is demarcated in the south by a line from Nose of Howth here across to Carmel Head here, and to the north by a line from Ballyquintin Point here across the Mull of Galloway here.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.640
But these waters don't recognise boundaries.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.520
Most people count the channels at its top and tail as part and parcel of it.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.480
This is a small sea, bursting to break out.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:03.280
I've washed into Dublin on its tumultuous tides, caused by the very formation of the Irish Sea.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:15.280
Over 250 million years, tectonic plates have caused subsidence of the seabed between Ireland and mainland Britain.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:29.000
The sea as we know it today was formed by rising sea levels as major ice sheets melted over 10,000 years ago.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:37.480
If we look at my chart again, we can see the effects of the Irish Sea's glacial history.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:41.120
Its waters are trapped in a deep basin...here.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:54.680
Twice daily tides flood in from the south, pulled by the drag of the moon, but the narrow passage of the north channel prevents the waters from escaping.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:03.360
So they do a circuit of the Irish Sea, before trying to escape southwards again, where they collide head-on with the next incoming tide.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:06.720
The result is a complex tidal swirl.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.880
And, there's another side-effect, a unique seasonal phenomenon.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:24.480
It's called the Irish Sea gyre, and it's the lifeblood of these waters.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.680
I want to find out what this phenomenon is and how it works.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:38.240
'I'm meeting Dr Martin White and Dr Glenn Nolan 'from the University of Galway and Marine Institute.'

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.560
I think that's about 15 rpm.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.840
That's about what we want.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.880
'They've promised to demonstrate an oceanic gyre 'on dry land...'

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.320
This is one of the strangest contraptions I've ever seen set up on a beach.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.360
What exactly is a gyre?

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.720
OK, Nick.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:07.360
A gyre is a term, a common term, we use to describe an enclosed circulation pattern of ocean currents.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:16.560
In the case of the western Irish Sea, this is a circulation pattern which goes around a dome of dense cold water sat in the deep part of the western Irish Sea here, between us and the Isle of Man.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:27.960
The idea to have this spinning is, if you think about the earth, everything is spinning around at the same rate, so we need to set that up first.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:37.520
As the earth turns, so does everything on it - including our oceans.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:44.120
We see this effect when water drains down a plughole.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.600
This circular flow is known as the Coriolis effect.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.000
How is your contraption going to demonstrate how the gyre works?

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:58.760
The green dye contained in the cylinder is dense water and swimming around in the main bowl we have fresh water.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.080
So when we release this, we'll create that dense dome in the middle and a cyclonic circulation around that.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.360
Can we try it?

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:20.280
The green dye is the cold dome of water, warmer clear water is swirling around it.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:28.320
At the boundary where the two meet, a current is generated, creating an invisible barrier.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.600
That is the gyre.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.600
Martin adds red dye so we can see the gyre current.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:44.039
The red dye's hanging like a curtain wrapping itself around the dome.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.080
This is small scale.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:54.720
Out in the deepest basin of the Irish Sea, the gyre spans 3,600 square miles.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:09:05.280
This invisible current creates a unique eco-system... ..an enclosed feeding ground, where fish and shellfish thrive.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:15.240
This sea's gyre creates rich pickings, and not just fish.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.760
Swirling currents can also bring tides of change.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:37.000
Religions, beliefs, cultures, carried around the Irish Sea... ..transforming our isles over thousands of years.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:44.920
The Romans were no strangers to these waters, for them a conduit to coastal conquest.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.560
In the first century, they reached Maryport, Cumbria and named it Alauna.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:57.280
It became a key garrison of the Roman Empire.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:09.320
Mark first came here ten years ago, when archaeologists discovered a huge military fort and settlement.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:18.360
Now he's back because an astonishing addition to the story has been unearthed - Roman temples.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:24.240
Maryport wasn't just a military fortress.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.840
It had a spiritual side, as well.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:34.040
I'm on a quest to discover why this outpost of the Roman empire was so important as a religious centre.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:52.920
Archaeologists here have uncovered two spectacularly rare temples, one round, probably a mausoleum and another intriguing rectangular structure.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.040
Tony Wilmott of Historic England is co-director of the project.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:00.880
This is the so-called rectangular temple.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.400
We've just got this end exposed.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:10.280
You've just got this side wall running there, then it comes out into this little square projection, then the facade was just about under the edge of grass area over there.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.760
Facing the same way as the other one?

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.200
We found, on excavating it properly, that there were four column bases.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:22.600
It would have had the lot - columns, pediment, the whole business.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:22.760
That's really unusual.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.640
You don't get classical temples.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.040
Not very often.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:30.400
Actually this is the furthest northwest classical temple in the Roman Empire so far.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.920
It's a staggering discovery.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.200
This was no everyday temple.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:45.400
It's size and quality suggests Maryport was a cult centre, drawing people from far and wide to worship.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:52.520
And there's stunning evidence to back this up in the local museum.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:09.920
In this museum is the largest collection of Roman altars in Britain, 18 in total and all found here at Maryport.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:17.760
As pagans, the Romans worshipped many gods and spirits.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:29.280
These exquisite altars were dedicated to Jupiter Optimus Maximus, the king of the gods.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.000
They probably would have stood in front of the temples.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:39.520
But these altars were dug up 100 metres away from the temples.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:48.680
At some point, they had been moved to an area where the team discovered a series of mysterious pits.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:53.200
Professor Ian Haynes of Newcastle University is the dig's co-director.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:13:00.720
These pits were actually dug to allow for a large timber structure to be built on this spot.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:07.560
Timber posts set up to 1.3 metres into the ground and packed around with stone, including sometimes altars.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:12.760
So they reused the altars as packing.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.640
Absolutely.

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:20.320
So if we look at this one, you can actually see the packing stone that's gone into one of these pits.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.360
And at the bottom of it, there, you can see the top of another altar.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:27.520
Once that's been taken away, yet again there it is - Jupiter Optimus Maximus.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:36.200
'What was this extraordinary structure 'that used Roman altars as building material?

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:39.280
'Clues were revealed by the excavations.'

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.320
What's that at one end?

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:43.680
Well, absolutely, this is key here, as you rightly point out.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:49.120
We've got an apse here, there's no doubt about it.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:56.800
There is a very clear semi-circular feature as one would find on the end of certain types of basilicum buildings.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.040
Well, basilicums can be churches.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:57.680
They can be, indeed.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:02.440
'This is another astounding discovery.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:11.480
'It seems the Roman altars 'had become the foundations 'for a colossal Christian church.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:18.640
'In the 1st century, 'the Romans invaded Britain as emphatic pagans.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:26.120
'Yet, within 300 years, 'they would destroy the symbols of that belief 'and embracing a brand-new faith.'

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:36.880
Christianity spread throughout the Empire and by the beginning of the 3rd century AD it may well have reached Roman Britain.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.400
Not that the Roman authorities approved.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:50.400
The faith of the first English martyr, St Alban, sealed his fate in the late 3rd century.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:15:03.480
In 314 AD, the Emperor Constantine adopted Christianity as the official religion of the Roman Empire and the first public churches were built.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:19.760
This extraordinary building unearthed at Maryport dates to the late 4th or early 5th century, one of the earliest churches ever discovered in Britain.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:31.960
We now realise that Maryport sits in the centre of a whole cluster of early Christian sites.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:35.280
Across the Solway was Whithorn, just behind the wind farm over there.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:39.120
Across the Irish Sea, we have the Isle of Man.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:44.360
Then beyond that, Nendrum in Northern Ireland and Iona in the Scottish islands.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:57.040
The emergence of Maryport as a Christian powerhouse in the north of England adds serious weight to a theory that's intrigued me for years.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:01.960
Because just 30 miles from here, a famous saint was born.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:11.760
This man - Saint Patrick, the apostle to the Irish.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:31.680
According to his own life, a document known as the Confession, Patrick says that he was born in a town called Bannaventa, which I think is this place, which the Romans called Glannaventa.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:44.680
Glannaventa is so close to Maryport it raises the possibility that St Patrick had a physical connection with this spiritual stretch of the Cumbrian coast.

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:56.160
If St Patrick was indeed from this place, it may well be from this very spot that he set off and took Christianity across the Irish Sea to the Irish.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:09.599
Romans established a pagan centre of worship here at Maryport on the edge of the Irish Sea.

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:19.560
With the arrival of Christianity and St Patrick, there came a new sea of faith, creating fresh sites of pilgrimage on our isles.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.680
The Irish Sea.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:43.800
Awash with opportunities that connect our coasts and our fishing ports.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.680
New Quay in Wales...

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.280
..Whitehaven in England...

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:53.920
..Portpatrick in Scotland...

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:01.880
This small sea binds together a big industry.

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:09.320
Dublin Bay is famous for one particular catch... ..the Dublin Bay Prawn.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.200
A product of the swirling gyre current.

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:22.640
Over 16,000 tonnes are landed in Ireland each year... ..but this crunchy crustacean knows no boundaries.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:29.640
Stocks are spreading throughout the Irish Sea... and have reached Belfast Lough.

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:38.280
It's where I've come to find out more about this delicacy, exported and eaten on our shores.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:41.880
But the name's a bit of a red herring.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:54.960
Because these prawns are also known as scampi and langoustine... ..which is less of a mouthful than their scientific name, Nephrops norvegicus.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:19:06.640
I'm heading out with the Agri-Food and Biosciences Institute, whose experts monitor Nephrops stocks for the Government.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:13.360
Principal scientist Richard Gowen has plotted on a chart how far the gyre spreads them.

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:23.600
The main population is retained here because of the physical environment, but, as these arrows show, when the Nephrops larvae are in the surface waters they will be dispersed elsewhere.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:41.400
There's a population here in the eastern Irish Sea, there's a population in the Clyde, and the general flow through the Irish Sea is from the south, through, so the small populations like the one here in Belfast Loch are spin-offs from the main population centres.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:42.200
Cousins of the gyre population.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.000
Yes.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:51.360
'The gyre is the optimum environment 'to nurture newly hatched Nephrops.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:56.440
'It retains most of the larvae and the plankton they feed on.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:20:04.640
'When larvae reach a certain size, 'they drop back to seabed burrows 'to metamorphose into adults.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:14.240
'Until now, I've never been up close and personal with a Nephrops... '..unlike senior scientist Steven Beggs.'

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.560
Wow, they're enormous!

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:21.440
Yes, these are big Dublin Bay prawns, Nick.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:27.600
Dublin Bay prawns or Nephrops can live up to 15 years.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.760
These feisty examples are about five.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.840
Ooh!

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.840
They're dangerous, aren't they?

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.000
Yeah, they can give you a nasty nip.

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:37.360
Would you like to feel the weight of it?

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.160
Sure.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.000
Absolutely stunning.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:51.440
Translucent pinks and oranges, with these very fine, fine fronds on the back of the tail, and this overlapping exoskeleton down the back here.

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:52.320
It's like armour plating on an armadillo.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:54.640
Yes, indeed.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:59.800
And they're very muscular - I've noticed that, flicking around, they've got a lot of power, very beautiful.

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:01.240
I'm going to put him back in.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:01.560
There you go.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:07.200
And just take me through their very complicated bodies.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:12.600
Yeah, they have this triple-fanned heel, which they use for propulsion.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:23.120
They are decapods - they have ten legs and they have these large front claws, which they use for scavenging and protecting themselves.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:30.720
But their claws are little protection against one predator - man.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:38.960
To prevent overfishing and to set quotas, it's vital the team know how many of these colourful crustaceans live in the Irish Sea.

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:42.600
A camera sledge records them.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:49.000
Towed behind the ship, it's designed to slide over the soft sea floor.

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:49.560
On the bottom!

00:21:52.720 --> 00:22:01.920
'Pictures are fed to the ship's computers, 'where principal scientist Matt Service has the unenviable task 'of counting each burrow.'

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:06.160
So this is a live feed from the seabed and the sledge shot we've just deployed.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:09.160
There's no way that I'd be able to spot a burrow in that!

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.160
It looks like green soup.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:13.720
Can you show me a picture of a burrow close-up so I know what to look for?

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:13.840
Yes.

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.160
Here we are.

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:22.440
Classically it's got this crescent shape at the front and then an escape entrance at the back.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:23.680
That must be so difficult.

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:26.840
That means you can't count an escape entrance, only the front door.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:31.280
That's easy - you've got one or two burrows, but it's not all that easy.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:31.800
So if we just click through a few slides...

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:39.560
Here's a shot from the middle of the Irish Sea, and you can see it's a lot of burrows, not so easy now.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:43.080
This is a bit like breaking open a chocolate bar and seeing all the bubbles.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:51.200
'And to make things more complicated, 'not every hole is a Nephrops burrow.'

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:51.880
So can I have a go at counting, Matt?

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:53.320
On you go.

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:53.680
Is that one?

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:58.480
One, two, three... four...

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:59.120
Yeah.

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:00.600
Just missed one there, I think.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:01.280
Oh, dear.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.600
Five...

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:05.880
There you go, six, seven!

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:06.200
That was one, wasn't it?

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:07.560
Oh, my eyeballs are getting tired already.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.040
Nine...

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.000
It's really difficult.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.000
It's really difficult.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:17.640
We would do this for maybe five minutes on a go, but you're getting the point, it can be done.

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:20.400
How many burrows have you counted in a single shift?

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:23.000
I guess probably...oh, certainly thousands.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:32.480
But if you look at the Irish Sea as a whole, we reckon that in 2013, the figure was 4.3 billion Nephrops burrows.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:33.160
4.3 billion burrows in the Irish Sea?

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:34.400
Yes.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.240
That's mind-boggling.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:44.480
The burrows are complex structures offering protection from predators.

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:53.160
Each one a bio-engineering marvel that oxygenates the sediment and regenerates minerals.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:54.720
They're also a natural work of art.

00:23:56.320 --> 00:24:04.120
Although it's flat boring mud on the surface, when you get below it, it's a bit of a complex.

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:13.200
So although the camera is seeing lots and lots of little burrow entrances, beneath those entrances, the entire Irish Sea is sort of excavated by these tiny little prawns.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:13.880
Yeah.

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:14.680
Absolutely amazing.

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:28.360
In the depths of the Irish Sea, the humble Nephrops is a crucial part of a vibrant eco-system, the gyre its lifeblood.

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:35.240
Thanks to the burrow counters, the flourishing future for Nephrops is one we all share.

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:48.960
Life thrives in the whirling currents of the Irish Sea.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:54.480
And so do stories.

00:24:53.560 --> 00:25:05.200
As people cross from coast to coast, they swap songs and traditions... ..tales and superstitions.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:10.600
These wild waters create legends of their own.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:18.080
They swirl around the sea's Celtic heart, ..the Isle of Man.

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:36.320
Singer and story teller Ruth Keggin recalls the tale of the mythical monarch of this magical place, the Sea God Manannan.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:58.120
Manannan Mac y Leir was a sea god, a king, a warrior, known in the Celtic nations of Ireland, Scotland and Wales.

00:25:57.320 --> 00:26:02.120
And, legend has it, he was the first ruler of the Isle of Man.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:11.240
Manannan lived in a castle at the summit of South Barrule, which is one of the high peaks of the Isle of Man in the south of the island.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:16.600
And, standing up at the top, you really get a sense of the island, with the sea all around you.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:42.880
Some of the people of the Isle of Man would travel up to the top of South Barrule on Midsummer's eve in order to pay a straw tax to Manannan of rushes.

00:26:42.040 --> 00:27:04.160
Part of what really appeals to me about Manannan is the idea that he was a shape-shifter, a magician, he was able to protect the island, not just with a sword, but by making one man appear like 100 men and by sending down a cloak of mist to shroud the island to defend it.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:21.320
Manannan's very name is associated with the sea, and you can think of it as his realm.

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:33.000
He was supposed to have had a great horse named Embar of the Flowing Mane, who was able to ride just as well over the waves as over the land.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:47.840
I think the story of Manannan has to have travelled across the Irish Sea.

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:48.560
When you think about it, the sea was a pathway.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:55.880
We know this because Manannan is known in other places apart from the Isle of Man.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:28:09.280
According to the myth, Manannan no longer resides on the summit of South Barrule, but instead lives on an underwater island 15 or 16 miles off the most southerly tip of the Isle of Man.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:19.320
And who knows, perhaps he's there still...

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:42.400
Coast is exploring how the Irish Sea touches us all and shapes our islands' story.

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:55.520
Cargoes carried across these waters have built cities.

00:28:54.720 --> 00:29:04.000
Granite from the Mountains of Mourne paved Liverpool... ..today a megaport.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:17.360
30 million tonnes of freight sails in each year, while leviathan cruise ships docking at Holyhead have made this place an unlikely tourist trap.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:26.120
The Irish Sea is a highway connecting our shores.

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:32.240
But sometimes what washes in can change the bonds between our coasts.

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:38.680
In Ireland's capital, Tess is investigating a troubled relationship...

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:47.320
Dublin, a coastal city linked to Britain by the Irish Sea.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:49.880
But when it comes to politics, things haven't always been plain sailing.

00:29:51.440 --> 00:30:03.560
After generations of demands for Irish independence, the pivotal event that would eventually achieve it erupted here, on Easter Monday, 1916.

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:11.120
At noon, a group of Irish nationalists seize a number of official buildings.

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:17.760
They call on fellow Irishmen to resist the bonds of British control and establish an independent Irish Republic.

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:21.720
After six days of fighting, 450 are dead and thousands injured.

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:24.040
Dublin is brought to her knees.

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:35.880
The General Post Office became the nationalist rebels' target and HQ.

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:38.720
Here they hoisted the Republican flag and proclaimed independence.

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:50.280
Riddled with bullet-holes, it's where I'm meeting Dr Conor Mulvagh from University College Dublin.

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:53.040
So this is the spot, where we're standing, where it all kicked off?

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:53.960
Absolutely.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:57.240
The Republic, in 1916, was declared right in front of us here.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:31:00.240
So what we have here is a bullet hole.

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:02.080
We can see the clear entry point, and then the wide exit point.

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:07.640
This would've come from the south and travelled north, so we can see a large shattering.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.360
They're shooting from over there?

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:11.040
This was the British forces shooting from the south.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:19.480
It was the British who eventually won, but they executed the rebel leaders making them martyrs.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:20.760
So this was a catalyst for independent southern Ireland.

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:31.040
Irish nationalism probably has its most transformative, it's most immediate shift, in the weeks after the 1916 rising.

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:33.080
The Easter Rising was a watershed moment.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:38.880
A bloody armed battle, between rebel volunteers and British forces.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:49.560
But how had a war of words over the so-called Irish question become a war with weapons?

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:52.720
And where had the rebels got their guns?

00:31:53.440 --> 00:32:04.080
To find out, I need to go back two years in time, and head for Howth, just north of Dublin.

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:13.160
It's the 26th of July, 1914, a 49-foot pleasure yacht named Asgard sails into this harbour.

00:32:12.520 --> 00:32:17.640
On board is an illegal cargo - 900 of these...

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:24.160
Mauser single-shot rifles that went on to arm the rebels at the Easter Rising.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:28.040
I want to know where the guns came from.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:32.400
I'm meeting local sailor Pat Murphy.

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:41.240
He's studied the Asgard's gun-running mission, codenamed Picnic.

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:44.160
It was a 23-day journey.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:58.680
They left from North Wales on the 3rd of July, and they headed down the Irish Sea and then across the Bristol Channel, around Land's End, and they rendezvoused with the German tug the Gladiator off the Belgium coast.

00:32:57.880 --> 00:33:01.760
They picked up the guns off the German tug.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.920
Asgard took 900.

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:07.480
I mean, that must've been a challenge - getting that many guns onto a sailing boat.

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:08.360
Yes, it was.

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:12.800
They, in fact, had to unpack them all from the bales of straw.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:16.160
They were stowed up to two-and-a-half feet high, all over the boat.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:22.840
This was just weeks before the outbreak of the First World War, The sea must have been chock-a-block with naval vessels.

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:23.920
Wasn't there a fear they'd be spotted?

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:24.520
It was a big fear.

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:32.200
In fact, as they were sailing passed Devonport, they sailed right through the British Navy on exercise.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:33.280
Well, did nobody sort of think this is a bit odd?

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:34.640
Nobody, nobody.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:37.040
We have a picture taken from Asgard.

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:37.600
Here we are.

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:39.840
Right.

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:39.960
Look at that.

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:40.320
That's very menacing, isn't it?

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.080
Yes!

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:47.880
You've got the warships in the background, and the gloom, the sense of foreboding.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:53.680
They arrived into Howth harbour just outside Dublin, in broad daylight.

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:57.720
Awaiting them, 1,000 volunteers to unload the illegal haul.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:03.600
But it wasn't the martyrs of the Easter Rising who were delivering these guns.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:15.840
The ring leaders weren't obvious revolutionaries, they were well-connected pillars of the Anglo-Irish aristocracy, but they were still whole-hearted supporters of home rule for Ireland.

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:27.840
Erskine Childers, an English-born veteran of the Boer War, he'd had spectacular success a decade earlier as author of the first-ever spy novel Riddle of the Sands.

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:37.840
Ironically, in it, he warned of the threat to Britain from an increasingly powerful Germany.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:44.639
Now he was skippering his own yacht, bringing weapons from Germany to overturn British rule in Ireland.

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:59.520
His conversion was due in no small part to his wife, Molly Childers - an American-born writer, she was a staunch Irish nationalist with a distaste for Imperialism and England.

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:15.640
But the linchpin in the whole operation was Mary Spring Rice, a liberal Anglo-Irish aristocrat, born and bred in County Limerick, and a passionate advocate of Irish culture.

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:25.560
I'm meeting Mary's nephew, Charles Spring Rice, to find out how a landed lady of the gentry took centre stage in this revolutionary operation.

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:31.880
What part did she play in the mission itself?

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:41.760
She was partly to do with organising the money to fund the guns that they were going to collect, and she, also, was the person who first thought up the idea of using private yachts.

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:49.160
Because she felt they're more likely to escape detection if they took it in private boats - particularly, in Cowes Week, as they were going to be going passed that.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:53.160
One of her letters here talks about how she worked at finding the right boat.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:58.520
"I've heard from someone in Berlin who might be helpful.

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:59.880
"We have a bigger boat which might go under Childers' captaincy."

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:03.920
Right, so here she is sending off missives to all her contacts trying to get her...

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:07.680
All her contacts she's working, internationally, to try and find a solution.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.320
And succeeds.

00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:09.880
Yes.

00:36:08.960 --> 00:36:18.880
Mary Spring Rice's hard work drummed up £1,500 - the equivalent of £150,000 today.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:23.640
Enough to buy the guns, and fund the trip.

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:27.160
Republicans weren't the only ones arming themselves.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:35.760
Just months earlier Loyalist volunteers in Northern Ireland had also landed guns from Germany - 25,000 of them.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:38.080
The clock was ticking.

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:48.600
Asgard and her cargo lit a touchpaper that put the burning Irish question centre stage.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:57.120
Two years elapsed between the arrival and the firing of the guns at the Easter Rising.

00:36:56.200 --> 00:37:02.880
Because nine days after the weapons came to this coast World War One was declared.

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:11.280
And under cover of war, the rebels had time to plan their strike.

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:20.440
So what did Asgard mean to those fighting for an independent Ireland?

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:23.400
Was her role forgotten in the passage of time?

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:31.200
To answer that, I've come to Kilmainham Gaol.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:36.360
Here Easter Rising rebels were held and executed.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:42.800
Oppressive cell walls are covered with political-prisoner graffiti.

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:43.680
It's just this cell here.

00:37:43.040 --> 00:37:49.760
Archaeologist Dr Laura McAtackney has unearthed one extraordinary scribbling.

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:51.520
It's very, very small, and very faint.

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:54.000
It's a little bit of pencil under whitewash.

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:56.680
So what do you think this is?

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:57.800
If you look at this picture here...

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:02.080
We think it's probably supposed to be the Asgard.

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:06.320
Yes.

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:11.840
And, actually, now you come to mention it, it does have the sort of flamboyant double-sail sketched on the wall.

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:12.200
Isn't it?

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:16.080
It's very specific looking to the Asgard, we think.

00:38:15.320 --> 00:38:19.960
It could've even had a picture that it was replicating, because it's very detailed.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:25.120
And if it is the Asgard, what does that actually tell us?

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:30.360
Well, it links into the idea that Kilmainham Gaol was already becoming a nationalist icon.

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:36.120
So this is tying into that with another kind of nationalist icon linking to the gun-running in Howth.

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:43.480
For the people imprisoned and killed in their fight for independence, Asgard had become the symbol of their struggle.

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:49.680
She's now at the National Museum of Ireland.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:58.960
It is incredible to finally see this 28-tonne yacht, named the Harbinger of Liberty by Republicans.

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:16.440
For some, Asgard started and epitomised a bloody conflict that rumbles on to this day.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:21.360
For others, this vessel is the symbol of independence and the struggle to achieve it.

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:33.400
Either way, this is an exquisite yacht clearly not built for military purposes, but which facilitated a mission with revolutionary intent.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:40.240
One voyage, one boat that changed the course of Irish and British history.

00:39:44.720 --> 00:40:00.160
Coast is exploring the Irish Sea... ..a small body of water that touches and binds our nations.

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:07.840
Ferries ply a profitable trade across this sea...

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:10.400
..Dublin to Holyhead...

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:21.160
..Cairnryan to Larne... ..but some have found novel ways to cross from coast to coast.

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:29.960
It's nothing new.

00:40:29.200 --> 00:40:34.680
This sea has long-inspired creative thinking.

00:40:34.320 --> 00:40:44.880
In the 18th century, plans were afoot at the tiny town of Whitehead, to permanently bridge the gap.

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:53.200
Without the haze, Scotland would be clearly visible across this 25-mile gulf between here and the Mull of Galloway.

00:40:54.720 --> 00:41:00.760
Linking these two coasts must surely have seemed possible.

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:04.600
I'm meeting Exeter University Professor Nick Groom.

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:10.560
He's studied some of the more eccentric ideas to bring our coasts together.

00:41:10.280 --> 00:41:15.240
In 1722, an anonymous satirist published a pamphlet called Thoughts of a Project for the Draining of the Irish Channel.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:21.320
Drain the Irish Sea?

00:41:20.360 --> 00:41:21.480
Drain the Irish Sea.

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:25.800
What do you think Britain and Ireland would have gained had we done away with all this water?

00:41:24.880 --> 00:41:37.440
The suggestion was to recover treasure from shipwrecks, also the seasoned timbers from shipwrecks that could then be used to build fishing traps for some of the larger sea creatures that are there.

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:37.720
And what sea creatures would we find?

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:44.680
Well, we're talking about "Sea-Goats, Kids, Apes, Baboons, "Sea-Bitches Babies, Foxes and their Cubs."

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:48.360
So you'd lose the sea and gain a land of opportunity.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:48.920
That's one of the implications of this pamphlet.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:54.400
It's to get its readers thinking about the definition of Britain, its relationship with Ireland.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:58.680
So the Irish Sea isn't necessarily something that separates, it's something which actually brings the isles together.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:11.800
The notion was tongue-in-cheek, but the desire to connect was deep-rooted.

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:17.080
It emerged again during the 1890s in another idea to link Britain and Ireland.

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:23.440
It would've made little Whitehead the mighty gateway to the Irish Sea.

00:42:25.360 --> 00:42:34.840
If I'd been sitting here in the early 1900s, I might have been right beside the railway tunnel entrance of a steam railway line emerging from beneath the Irish Sea.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:38.400
This is the front cover for a proposal for four railway tunnels.

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:40.680
They hadn't yet decided which one they wanted to build.

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:48.120
One of them went from Donaghadee to Portpatrick, the second one from Whitehead - where I'm sitting now - across to Portpatrick.

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:55.360
A third one, from Whitehead across to Stranraer, and a fourth one further north from Cushendun across the Mull of Kintyre.

00:42:57.680 --> 00:43:07.280
The tunnels were the brainchild of civil engineer Luke Livingston Macassey.

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:11.480
Recognising the Irish Sea's importance to trade, he wanted to tap into the ever-expanding railways.

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:22.160
This proposed line was linking places that were further afield, through trains between Edinburgh, Glasgow, Manchester, Belfast and Londonderry.

00:43:23.200 --> 00:43:28.480
Pros and cons were debated in Parliament.

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:35.800
Unfortunately, the grand plans came with a hefty price tag - up to £200 million.

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:36.400
The scheme was reluctantly shelved, so no tunnel.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:45.960
But generations have still made the crossing.

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:59.000
The Irish Sea is not so much a gulf, more a bridge connecting neighbours.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:10.680
It's long been a route to new shores, from those fleeing famine in 19th century Ireland, to those escaping further afield.

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:17.760
But these waters are tinged with tragedy.

00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:22.480
Lambay, off the coast of Dublin.

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:29.240
A sleeping giant whose Gaelic name is Island of the Shipwrecks.

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:35.560
It's lured Miranda and Cassie to investigate a coastal calamity that shocked Victorian society.

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:43.320
Behind me, looming out of the water, are the sheer and jagged cliffs of Lambay Island.

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:57.800
A 150 years ago, these rocks claimed an unsuspecting prey - a ship that was heralded as a symbol of the modern era, a ship that was on her maiden voyage, and a ship were hundreds of people died.

00:44:56.960 --> 00:45:06.840
This was the Royal Mail ship Tayleur, the Titanic of the Victorian age.

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:17.040
And like Titanic, the RMS Tayleur was hailed as the biggest, finest vessel in the world - pride of the White Star Line.

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:20.880
She was one of the first iron-hulled ships.

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:33.640
On-board for her maiden voyage in the winter of 1854, were 700 excited travellers - entire families immigrating to a new world, Australia.

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:40.560
But eager hopes for a new life were dashed.

00:45:39.760 --> 00:45:45.680
The Tayleur now rests in a watery grave 18 metres below the waves.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:50.880
The murky details of her fateful voyage have never been fully pieced together.

00:45:52.160 --> 00:46:01.600
So I'm going to be heading into the water to investigate this enigmatic wreck to find out how much of her is left and see if I can find any clues as to why she sank.

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:10.240
Helping me by scrutinising the evidence on land is Cassie Newland.

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:14.640
I want to trace the ship's maiden voyage, so I'm starting where RMS Tayleur was built.

00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:20.920
An unlikely spot for ship-building, Warrington, 19 miles up the River Mersey.

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:26.200
It's where I'm meeting historian Gill Hoffs, who's spent years researching the story.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:35.280
So what's the Tayleur like?

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:36.440
She's glorious - look.

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:40.600
Can you imagine this beauty sailing down here?

00:46:46.040 --> 00:46:52.280
She was enormous, she was luxurious, and she was revolutionary.

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:58.920
She was one of the first iron clippers and she was meant to be the fastest, the safest and most splendid vessel afloat.

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:02.600
She had two flush toilets.

00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:04.080
In the 1850s?

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:04.800
In the 1850s.

00:47:03.840 --> 00:47:06.160
Oh, very posh!

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:09.040
The Tayleur made her way down the Mersey to Liverpool.

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:16.640
The burden of expectation on this supposedly safe ship, immense.

00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:24.760
She and her eager passengers left the coast amid great fanfare on the 19th of January, 1854.

00:47:23.920 --> 00:47:32.800
Just 48 hours into her maiden voyage, she was enveloped in thick mist and veering drastically off course, heading for Lambay.

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:33.840
"SAILOR": Land-ho on the lee bow!

00:47:37.400 --> 00:47:44.760
Swept towards the island, winter winds caught the sails.

00:47:43.840 --> 00:47:48.320
Ropes jammed around the rigging and the mast.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:51.240
The crew were unable to pull in the sails against the power of the wind.

00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:55.200
The rocks rose like a mountain out of the middle of the sea, as the tide pushed them inland.

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:03.240
The captain ordered the anchors to be dropped, the chains snapped like glass.

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:09.480
The mid-section smashed into the rocks, and she started sinking by the stern within minutes.

00:48:08.640 --> 00:48:17.040
At this time the scene was one of the most appalling description, people ran wildly to-and-fro uttering the most piercing cries of distress.

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:26.040
One man convinced hope was lost, wrote a message in a bottle.

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:29.840
"Many passengers and crew are now drowning before my eyes, "and there's no assistance.

00:48:28.960 --> 00:48:31.240
"My wife is also lost."

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:36.400
Around 380 passengers and 30 crew perished.

00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:45.120
Cassie's got hold of the ship's plans and cargo list to investigate the disaster.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:50.800
I want to find out why the Tayleur sank when she's supposed to be a cutting-edge ship.

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:56.760
And I'm hoping my interest in Victorian engineering is going to help me do that.

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:59.480
Weaknesses are clear immediately.

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:04.640
The wide gap between the masts would affect the centre of gravity and, therefore, handling.

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:10.520
The tiny rudder is built for speed not sharp manoeuvre.

00:49:10.040 --> 00:49:18.440
But I think the key factor is what the boat's made of - it's an iron hull, and we know that that can disrupt your compass readings.

00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:21.480
To work out how much, I'm doing a little experiment.

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:24.680
Now this is a compass as we know it - it points north.

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:25.720
But it's a very simple system.

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:37.040
All you need is a magnetised needle like this one, on a freely rotation axis which we can make like this.

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:38.080
And with any luck...

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:38.960
Yeah - it's really simple - it's now pointing north.

00:49:38.560 --> 00:49:45.000
The iron in our magnetised needle is drawn to the strongest magnetic force.

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:49.280
In this case, that is the natural magnetic force created by the Earth.

00:49:48.600 --> 00:50:00.800
So, now, if we take these chains and pretend that they are the iron hull of the boat and wrapping it around the bowl... the needle moves.

00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:01.120
It's not pointing to the north any more.

00:50:00.520 --> 00:50:11.040
Instead, it's drawn to the iron that its nearest to it, and if you think about the hull of the Tayleur - that is 1,750 tonnes of iron, the deflection can be quite substantial, and that's very dangerous.

00:50:13.240 --> 00:50:21.680
It was a well-known issue, and iron-clad ships usually re-set their compasses to compensate.

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:22.480
But the Tayleur had an additional problem.

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:35.400
More iron was loaded into the ship in the form of cargo - things like great coils of barbed wire, plough shears, pots and pans...

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:39.120
And most importantly for our disaster, a full-sized river ship.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:44.720
The compass, vital on the boat, is no longer pointing north.

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:53.120
It might only be a few degrees out, but when you're crossing the Irish Sea it's the difference between going down here, or ending up on the rocks here.

00:50:55.040 --> 00:51:07.240
We've got special permission to dive the wreck and I'm hoping that I can find out just a little bit more about her by getting a bit closer.

00:51:06.320 --> 00:51:11.760
Today the wreck is a protected site, so nothing can be removed or disturbed.

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:21.800
It's so eerie down here.

00:51:20.920 --> 00:51:23.000
Very poor visibility.

00:51:24.400 --> 00:51:26.880
Oh, I see the first bit of wreckage.

00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:33.640
The ship's obviously lying on her side.

00:51:37.120 --> 00:51:40.400
This is the site of one of the portholes.

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:45.000
Yes, look at this.

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:49.480
This is the anchor chain.

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:50.560
Look at the size of the links there!

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:57.480
Something that thick, actually snapped.

00:51:56.760 --> 00:52:01.080
That is how powerful the winds and the waves and the tides were on the day she was wrecked.

00:52:03.800 --> 00:52:15.800
This ship was carrying hundreds of young families, who'd eagerly packed all their worldly goods.

00:52:15.680 --> 00:52:20.560
Can you see these stacked one on top of another?

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:20.920
These are roof slates, believe it or not!

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:23.840
So they were travelling with all the equipment they'd need to set-up a new life.

00:52:24.520 --> 00:52:32.480
Chillingly, they even took their own gravestones.

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:35.440
And it feels very spooky.

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:38.080
Oh, my God, there's a bit of pottery!

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:40.520
Look at this!

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:43.240
It's just here...

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:49.280
I can't touch it, but just here... you can see the white of a piece of pottery.

00:52:48.520 --> 00:52:52.160
Now, that's some sort of bowl or maybe the top of a jug.

00:52:54.280 --> 00:52:59.080
It's all just coming alive now.

00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:16.560
Underwater I can only get tantalising glimpses of the cargo, so local diver Harry Breslin who was among the first ever to explore the wreck, has brought some stunning artefacts to show me.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:18.840
SHE GASPS Yeah, so this is, erm...

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:19.400
Oh, my goodness!

00:53:18.640 --> 00:53:20.600
A little pepper canister.

00:53:19.920 --> 00:53:21.040
Look at that... !

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:23.360
That's absolutely beautiful.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:26.520
And it's perfectly intact.

00:53:25.640 --> 00:53:27.440
Yes, indeed.

00:53:26.920 --> 00:53:30.480
I like to feel it's something special, you know, and, er...

00:53:29.720 --> 00:53:33.680
It's a real personal thing, isn't it?

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:34.960
A personal item.

00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:38.000
It sat on their table, this is what they used to flavour their food with.

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:38.520
Indeed.

00:53:37.760 --> 00:53:39.760
Oh, look at that.

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:42.120
I mean, that looks like you've just bought it from the shop, doesn't it?

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:44.760
That is absolutely beautiful.

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:46.440
There's not a chip or a scratch on it.

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:58.400
These incredible finds are a direct connection with the ordinary families on board, all with their own stories.

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:01.520
But one statistic baffles me.

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:07.720
Just 3% of women and children survived this tragedy, compared with 70% of men.

00:54:06.760 --> 00:54:09.360
Why?

00:54:08.720 --> 00:54:12.240
Author Gill Hoffs has a theory.

00:54:12.440 --> 00:54:21.320
The clothing that women wore at that time was very heavy, it was incredibly restrictive, and they could wear at least 16 layers, easy.

00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:22.840
16 layers?!

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:24.480
And you'd be very constricted.

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:25.880
So are these the clothes that they would've worn?

00:54:24.960 --> 00:54:28.080
These are similar.

00:54:27.280 --> 00:54:34.240
This is a very, very heavy velvet overskirt.

00:54:33.400 --> 00:54:35.120
If you feel the weight of that.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:35.640
Oh!

00:54:34.960 --> 00:54:38.560
If you can imagine that drenched in water.

00:54:37.680 --> 00:54:42.960
And the thing is, on board the ship you'd maybe get to access your clothing maybe once a fortnight.

00:54:42.000 --> 00:54:45.800
Once a week if it was excellent weather.

00:54:45.400 --> 00:54:46.520
And it's January.

00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:46.720
Yes.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:47.160
And it's freezing.

00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:47.240
Yes.

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:48.400
And it's damp.

00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:48.520
Yes.

00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:51.280
I'd be wearing everything.

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:57.840
Passengers were advised to stitch valuables into their undergarments.

00:54:57.120 --> 00:55:02.560
One corset had 200 sovereigns sewn into it.

00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:03.840
Must weigh an absolute ton!

00:55:02.880 --> 00:55:05.600
A sovereign is, what, eight grams?

00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:05.920
Something like that?

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:07.720
It's quite a hefty coin, isn't it?

00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:08.360
It is a hefty coin.

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:08.960
Yes.

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:10.720
It's like wearing a diver's weight belt.

00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:13.680
One woman had £3,000.

00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:14.200
Yes.

00:55:13.400 --> 00:55:16.160
She perished.

00:55:15.800 --> 00:55:26.000
These tragic losses appalled a Victorian society which championed the notion of "women and children first".

00:55:25.040 --> 00:55:30.640
But even if they had gone first, did their clothing condemn them to their deaths?

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:33.040
I've volunteered to test the theory.

00:55:32.160 --> 00:55:38.960
My Victorian dress is heavy and layered, and I've added a weight-belt.

00:55:38.520 --> 00:55:43.920
But I'm wearing a wetsuit underneath and have two lifeguards on standby.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:48.000
That said, I'm still petrified!

00:55:55.040 --> 00:56:03.280
Actually, moving anywhere, in a direction, is really hard cos you're just a giant sail.

00:56:04.640 --> 00:56:15.440
I always think of myself as a very strong swimmer, but when it starts getting caught around your feet it makes you panic cos you can't do what you'd normally do to save yourself.

00:56:15.240 --> 00:56:18.880
It's like dragging an anchor.

00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:19.880
It's really knackering.

00:56:21.520 --> 00:56:28.160
As time ticks by, it's ever harder to stay afloat.

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:30.160
I wouldn't be able to keep this up for more than a few minutes.

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:50.840
You really feel for them as humans and families, because there is so little you could possibly do to rescue the situation, and some of them are travelling with 12 kids, and there's... there's no way in hell...

00:56:50.120 --> 00:56:55.000
There's no way in hell you can physically do it - you're beaten.

00:56:54.200 --> 00:56:55.640
It's awful.

00:56:59.960 --> 00:57:03.840
Less than half the passengers managed to scramble ashore.

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:06.760
Only three women survived.

00:57:07.240 --> 00:57:14.840
An inquest concluded the ship was under-crewed, and White Star had made fatal mistakes.

00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:20.840
But it now appears there were many more reasons for this shipwreck, and her tragic death toll.

00:57:20.960 --> 00:57:28.040
A combination of factors sealed her fate - tides, winds, engineering...

00:57:27.400 --> 00:57:31.960
But whilst the wreck of the Tayleur remains on the seabed, at least the story is raised from the depths.

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:48.400
I've reached the end of my journey up the western Irish Sea coast.

00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:51.880
But it's not quite the end of my time on this sea.

00:57:51.040 --> 00:57:56.680
I'm catching a ferry back to another coast connected by this water.

00:57:55.840 --> 00:58:01.320
I've visited Wales, Ireland, Northern Ireland - I'm finishing bound for Scotland.

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:17.240
The Irish Sea touches us in surprising ways, and defines our island story.

00:58:16.280 --> 00:58:29.520
A sea that takes as it provides people and ideas still carried on the tides and currents of these waters.

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:57.400
Like the gyre, the Irish Sea continues to swirl with identities and connections supporting life old and new - produce, culture, tragedy and change have all washed across the Irish Sea from coast to coast.

00:59:05.960 --> 00:59:08.880
Perhaps we're not so much a collection of different countries sharing the Irish Sea, but, rather, people OF the Irish Sea.

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:35.880
This... is the Atlantic... the wildest ocean on Earth.
